<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article article-type="letter"><?properties open_access?><!-- Original-type: sc--><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">11720481</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2375244</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6692083</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1054/bjoc.2001.2083</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Short Communication</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>New cytotoxic benzo(b)thiophenilsulfonamide 1,1-dioxide derivatives inhibit a NADH oxidase located in plasma membranes of tumour cells</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Alonso</surname><given-names>M M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Enc&#x000ed;o</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Mart&#x000ed;nez-Merino</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Gil</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Migliaccio</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>Department of Health Sciences, Public University of Navarra, Avd Bara&#x000f1;a&#x000ed;n s/n, Pamplona, 31008, Spain</aff><aff id="aff2"><label>2</label>Department of Applied Chemistry, Public University of Navarra, Campus de Arrosad&#x000ed;a, Pamplona, 31006, Spain</aff><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>01</day><month>09</month><year>2001</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>11</month><year>2001</year></pub-date><volume>85</volume><issue>9</issue><fpage>1400</fpage><lpage>1402</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>24</day><month>04</month><year>2001</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>03</day><month>07</month><year>2001</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>18</day><month>07</month><year>2001</year></date></history><copyright-statement>Copyright 2001, Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2001</copyright-year><permissions><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>A series of benzo(b)thiophenesulfonamide 1,1-dioxide derivatives (BTS) have been designed and synthesized as candidate antineoplastic drugs. Several of these compounds have shown in vitro cytotoxic activity on leukaemic CCRF-CEM cells. The cytotoxic BTS, but not the inactive ones, were able to inhibit a tumour cell-specific NADH oxidase activity present in the membrane of CCRF-CEM cells. &#x000a9; 2001 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>diarylsulfonylureas</kwd><kwd>antineoplasic drugs</kwd><kwd>NADH oxidase</kwd><kwd>CCRF-CEM</kwd><kwd>plasma membrane</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>